Localisation (LOC):

LOC 01:
A] The robot should determine global position using GNSS when satellite data is available
B] The robot should estimate local motion using odometry
C] The robot should measure heading using magnetometer
D] The robot should measure acceleration and angular rate using an IMU
E] The robot should gracefully combine aforementioned sensor info to produce a unified localisation estimate.

LOC 02:
A] The robot should account for differing update rates between sensors
B] The robot shall determine a method to weight different sensor readings based on availability and reliability and put into a filter

LOC 03:
A] The robot should update localisation estimates at a minimum defined frequency of X Hz

LOC 04:
A] The robot should output position, velocity, and orientation back to ground computer
B] The robot should provide localisation data to the navigational and control systems

LOC 05:
A] The robot should detect a loss of GNSS signal
B] The robot should continue estimating position using IMU and odometry based on the previous available GNSS reading
C] The robot should recognise degraded localisation accuracy
D] The robot should maintain a positional accuracy of X metres.

Software (SOF):

SOF 01:
A] The robot should execute onboard software to manage and combine sensing, control, communication and logging
B] The robot should initialize required software services during startup
C] The robot should verify successful startup of critical software components
D] The robot should enter a safe, predetermined state if critical software or hardware components fail to start

SOF 02:
A] The robot should separate software into distinct categories (e.g. sensing, localisation, control, communication and data logging)
B] The robot should allow new hardware modules to be supported by adding it’s corresponding software drivers.
C] The robot should allow software modules to be independently updated for isolated troubleshooting

SOF 03:
A] The robot should validate sensor data before analysis, logging or communication, the extent of which is TBD

SOF 04:
A] The robot should provide a software interface for communication between functional modules
B] The robot should ensure that failure in non critical software does not crash the entire system

SOF 05:
A] The robot should store configurable software parameters for sensors, localisation, communication and control
B] The robot should allow configuration parameters to be modified extremely easily
C] The robot should maintain an configuration log with saved configurations for quick and easy access.

SOF 06:
A] The robot should provide status information to the operator
B] The robot should provide a user friendly experience for the ground computer and should be able to be ran with low cost controllers.
C] The robot should maintain version information for deployed software components
D] The robot should support future addition of new sensors and mobility modules without major software redesign

Navigation (NAV):

NAV 01- Location tracking:
A] The system shall be able to determine its geographic location and update it at defined time intervals
B] The system shall record location data alongside sensor data
C] The system shall store location data locally when communication is unavailable
D] The system shall maintain a history of recorded locations for later retrieval

Nav 02- Location reporting:
A] The system shall transmit its current or last-known location to an external system
B] The system shall include location data in all transmitted datasets.
C] The system shall provide location data during both normal and fault conditions.

Nav 03- Emergency location and retrieval support:
A] The system shall transmit its last known location upon entering abort mode.
B] The system shall prioritise location transmission during critical power conditions.
C] The system shall retain location data during system shutdown. 
D] The system shall provide sufficient location data to enable physical retrieval.
E] The system shall enable identification of the system during recovery operations. 
Control (CON):

CON 01- Control philosophy:
A] The system shall first be designed for certain sensors and movement to be completely user controlled and later optimised to be primarily autonomous with user interaction allowed for overriding certain functions
B] The system shall automatically manage sensor operation based on configured parameters unless indicated otherwise
C] One raspberry pi acts as the main computer and an arduino controls the sensors and feed it back into the raspberry pi to receive instructions

CON 02- User control capabilities during operation: 
A] The user shall be able to stop and start system operation 
B] The user shall be able to place the system into standby or low-power mode.
C] The user shall be able to initiate a system reset.
D] The user shall be able to manually trigger an abort mode.
E] The user shall be able to request immediate data transmission.
F] The user shall be able to place the system into a safe shutdown state. 
CON 03- System feedback and communication:
A] The system shall provide status feedback for all control actions.
B] The system shall report current system state (e.g. active, standby, fault).
C] The system shall provide confirmation of configuration changes.
D] The system shall receive and process control commands from an external device.
E[ The system shall confirm execution of received commands. 

CON 04- Control verification:
A] The system shall validate all received control commands before execution.
B] The system shall reject invalid or unsafe commands.
C] The system shall prevent user commands from causing system damage or data loss.

Power (POW):

POW 01- Power supply details:
A] The system shall be powered by an internal rechargeable lithium-ion battery system
B] The system shall operate using a nominal supply voltage between X V and Y V.
C] The system shall provide regulated power outputs dependent on the module
D] The system shall include voltage regulation circuitry to ensure stable operation of all components.

POW 02- Power distribution:
A] The system shall distribute power from a central power management unit to the core and module components
B] The system shall prevent voltage fluctuations from affecting sensor or processing performance.
C] The system shall isolate critical components from non-critical loads where possible. 
POW 03- Power monitoring:
A] The system shall continuously monitor battery voltage.
B] The system shall estimate remaining battery capacity based on voltage levels.
C] The system shall monitor internal temperature for safety purposes.
D] The system may monitor current draw to detect abnormal power usage.

POW 04- Power state management:
A] The system shall prioritise data preservation during all low-power states. 
B] The system shall operate with defined power states based on battery level.
C] The system shall transition between normal, warning, and critical power modes. (EMRG 02)
D] In normal mode all systems operate normally
E] In warning mode sensor sampling frequency and communication frequency are reduced to conserve power
F] In critical mode non-essential components including non-essential sensors will be stopped to prevent corruption and prioritise data storage and transmission

POW 05- Battery safety features:
A] The system shall include battery protection circuitry (BMS).
B] The system shall prevent overcharging and over-discharging of the battery.
C] The system shall protect against short circuits and excessive current draw.
D] The system shall shut down safely under unsafe electrical conditions.

Emergencies (EMRG):

EMRG 01- Data emergency features:
A] The system stores data locally before transmitting 
B] Recording continues regardless of connection status
C] Data is uploaded automatically immediately after signal return
D] Critical readings and timestamp logs are duplicated
E] The system shall adapt sampling and transmission rates during degraded conditions

EMRG 02- Power emergency features:
A] The system shall detect and report low battery conditions and send warnings at: 20%, 10% and 5% 
B] Warning mode commences at 10%
C] Shutdown starts at 5% by commencing pre-shutdown operations to reserve sufficient energy before carrying out the final data transmission during shutdown
D] Starting from 10% the system shall initiate protective behaviours including preventing battery over-discharge and initial power reserving

EMRG 03- Communication emergency features:
A] When signal is lost the system periodically attempts to auto-reconnecting
B] The system shall support alternative communications in case of a primary failure by attempting WiFi, LoRa and finally bluetooth
C] Periodic status signals are transmitted to confirm operational health
D] Signals of location are sent at shutdown and location data of launch and emergency contact can be accessed

EMRG 04- Health detection protocols:
A] The system shall perform periodic self-diagnostics to assess operational health
B] During fault conditions the system shall enter a reduced-function mode where non-essential sensors are stopped to prioritise data saving and transmission
C] Internal moisture ingress will be detected to trigger a fault state in the case of leaks
D] Overheating shall be detected then prevented by reducing or stopping operation under high internal temperatures

General Design (GEN):

GEN 01- Core design:
A] The core should be within a cylinder of dimension 300m x Ø100mm and external modules will have dimensions limits specific to each module
B] The core should weigh a maximum of 2.5 kg
C] The core should have waterproofing: IP67 minimum (ideally IP68\)
D] The core should maintain integrity under standard deployment conditions

GEN 02- Sustainable design:
A] The system should use reusable connectors and fasteners wherever possible
B] The system may support future integration of renewable energy sources such as solar charging
GEN 03- Journey details:
A] The system should clearly display information regarding vitals including battery power, connection status and internal temperature
B] The duration and power usage for one-way and return journeys should be calculated using a given route 

GEN 04- Mounting:
A] The system shall support sensor integration via sealed connectors or mounting ports
B] The end cap shall allow mounting of multiple sensors
C] The system shall support vertical or horizontal deployment orientations
D] The enclosure shall include mounting points or support external mounting mechanisms
E] The power supply should be easily removable for ease of recharging
Autonomous Operations (AUTO):

AUTO 1:
Whether battery conditions will be sufficient to complete the mission will be periodically calculated and if they are not sufficient, the system will either return or continue dependent on which is more optimal
